<template><div><section class="border-b border-ink/10 bg-white py-16 md:py-24"><div class="wrap max-w-4xl"><p class="label-mono text-vomtek">Standards · 8 min read</p><h1 class="type-page-hero mt-4 font-display leading-[.96] tracking-[-.05em]">How we check if a damaged pipe is still safe.</h1><p class="mt-5 text-[16px] text-ink/70">This is a safety check, not a replacement for an engineer’s judgement.</p></div></section><article class="section-y"><div class="wrap max-w-3xl space-y-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-ink/75"><p>The check estimates how much pressure a damaged pipe can still hold. It uses four simple inputs: pipe width, wall thickness, how deep the damage is and how long it is.</p><h2 class="pt-4 font-display text-[30px] text-ink">What Vomtek calculates</h2><p>For each problem, the system calculates a safe pressure limit and compares it with the pressure the pipe normally runs at. The result is ranked so you know what needs attention first.</p><div class="rounded-xl border border-ink/12 bg-bone p-5 font-mono text-[12px] leading-relaxed"><p>z = L² / (D · t)</p><p>M = √(1 + 0.6275z − 0.003375z²)</p><p>P′ = 2 · S · t / D · [(1 − 0.667 d/t) / (1 − 0.667(d/t)/M)]</p></div><h2 class="pt-4 font-display text-[30px] text-ink">Where teams go wrong</h2><p>Using depth alone, ignoring nearby damage, and comparing inspections without lining up the distance. Vomtek keeps the inputs, method and reviewer so the result can be checked.</p><p class="text-[13px] text-ink/75">This article is for general understanding. Ask a qualified engineer before changing pressure or approving a repair.</p></div></article></div></template>
